AVONET trait data
-----------------

Bundled objects: avonet300 and avonet_full.

These objects contain subsets and transformations of AVONET. The AVONET
article and data are released under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY)
licence. Users must retain attribution to the original authors.

Source and citation:

Tobias JA et al. (2022). AVONET: morphological, ecological and geographical
data for all birds. Ecology Letters 25:581-597.
doi:10.1111/ele.13898

Data archive: https://figshare.com/s/b990722d72a26b5bfead

Example bird phylogenies
------------------------

Bundled objects: tree300, trees300, and tree_full.

These objects are pruned derivatives of tree_bird_n100, retrieved through the
megatrees 1.0.0 R package. The package's MIT software licence does not
establish redistribution rights for the underlying BirdTree data. The exact
upstream release asset has SHA-256 digest
71f6f712bf4c57c14570644f447c1e2eb0d76436a88075560afd4e85be1fcb78.
That 100-tree BirdTree collection has 50 Ericson-backbone trees followed by 50
Hackett-backbone trees. tree300 and tree_full use posterior member 69 from the
Hackett-backbone set. trees300 is the deterministic seed-42 sample of 50 trees
from the full collection: 28 Ericson and 22 Hackett, each pruned to the same
300 AVONET species. These are posterior sample trees, not an MCC tree.

tree300 and tree_full were selected as example trees. Member 69 is tied for the
smallest Robinson-Foulds distance to the former example topology and
reproducibly retains the pre-existing AVONET safety-floor smoke criterion on
the CPU path. The trees300 sampling procedure is recorded in
data-raw/make_trees300.R.

Source and citation:

Ericson PGP et al. (2006). Diversification of Neoaves: integration of molecular
sequence data and fossils. Biology Letters 2(4):543-547.
doi:10.1098/rsbl.2006.0523

Hackett SJ et al. (2008). A phylogenomic study of birds reveals their
evolutionary history. Science 320(5884):1763-1768.
doi:10.1126/science.1157704

Jetz W, Thomas GH, Joy JB, Hartmann K, Mooers AO (2012). The global diversity
of birds in space and time. Nature 491:444-448.
doi:10.1038/nature11631

